  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nWe're in the midst of a tranquil stretch of weather. Very little has \nchanged since the last update. Skies are clear, and winds are calm. \nSprawling high pressure at the surface remains in place over the \neastern US and Canada. In the upper levels, a large ridge sits over \nthe central CONUS. \n\nThe mid/upper level ridge is forecast to slowly build eastward over \nthe Ohio Valley through the end of the weekend. The strong sfc high \nwill build south through the weekend, becoming centered over the \nNortheast US. During the early to middle portion of next week, \nridging will remain in place while gradually weakening with time. \nThe upper level flow deamplifies, and multiple upper level \ndisturbances will traverse portions of the western and north-central \nUS. While we could see increasing high clouds at times, a dry low-\nlevel airmass will keep us dry through next Thursday. \n\nIn short, very pleasant Fall weather continues through the middle of \nnext week. Afternoon highs in the low to mid 70s will be common, \nwith early morning lows in the 40s. Our coolest, sheltered valleys \nin the Bluegrass and Lake Cumberland regions should see some mid to \nupper 30s once again early this morning and again Saturday morning. \nThis may produce patchy frost in those areas. \n\nForecast confidence has increased slightly in our next chance of \nrain arriving over Halloween weekend. A wave of low pressure \nejecting northeast out of the southern/central Plains could finally \ndraw some moisture into the Ohio Valley. About 40-50% of LREF \nmembers bring us some measurable rainfall by Sunday, November 1st.",
